From e2d219540efbce0ab50d022640d7a0280e2ad0cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ramesh Goswami <ramesh@listenfirstmedia.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:29:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v0.4.0: replace exact-label SPARQL with variant-aware
 multilingual lookup

The exact-match query produced silent false negatives - 'A.R. Rahman' vs
'A. R. Rahman', and every non-Latin spelling - which is worse than a collision
because not_found reads as 'no Wikidata item'.

- labels.py: normalisation (initials, diacritics, honorifics, suffixes),
  variant generation, script detection -> search languages, fuzzy scoring with
  provenance (exact|normalized|initials|token|fuzzy|translit).
- wikidata.py: wbsearchentities for recall across variants and languages, then
  batched wbgetentities for precision - P31=Q5 humans only, label floor 0.62.
  Drops films and disambiguation pages that share a person's name.
- Transliteration is a weak signal only, capped at 0.75. Unidecode is far too
  lossy on Indic scripts to carry a matching decision; Wikidata's own
  multilingual labels do that work instead.
- Candidate.label_match records how the name matched, so a translit hit can be
  scrutinised differently from an exact one.
- Old exact-SPARQL path kept as discover_candidates_sparql for reference.

161/161 tests pass across three suites. No credentials in any file.
